\@writefile{lof}{\defcounter {refsection}{0}\relax }\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces The unrolled discriminator distributions for the jet-driven approach. These represent the CvsB discriminator, in three bins of the CvsL discriminator, for the selected jet of the reconstructed cH(ZZ$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rightarrow 4\mu $)\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}candidates. It is shown once inclusively in the Higgs boson candidate mass spectrum and once with Higgs boson candidate mass criteria applied. As seen in the lower panel of both figures, the relative contribution of a cH(ZZ$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rightarrow 4\mu )_{\kappa _c}$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}signal with respect to the background estimation generally increases with increasing bin number in both distributions. The region most enriched with signal are the right-most bins of both distributions. This is as expected, since these bins correspond to selected jets with both a high CvsL and CvsB value. Here, the cH(ZZ$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rightarrow 4\mu )_{\kappa _c}$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}signal contribution is scaled by a factor of $10^{2}$ to improve its visibility. Note the dashed line that represents the threshold where at least one, or more, events are expected per bin.\relax }}{76}{figure.caption.61}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:UnrolledDiscs}{{4.16}{76}{The unrolled discriminator distributions for the jet-driven approach. These represent the CvsB discriminator, in three bins of the CvsL discriminator, for the selected jet of the reconstructed \cHZZ candidates. It is shown once inclusively in the Higgs boson candidate mass spectrum and once with Higgs boson candidate mass criteria applied. As seen in the lower panel of both figures, the relative contribution of a \cHZZsen signal with respect to the background estimation generally increases with increasing bin number in both distributions. The region most enriched with signal are the right-most bins of both distributions. This is as expected, since these bins correspond to selected jets with both a high CvsL and CvsB value. Here, the \cHZZsen signal contribution is scaled by a factor of $10^{2}$ to improve its visibility. Note the dashed line that represents the threshold where at least one, or more, events are expected per bin.\relax }{figure.caption.61}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\defcounter {refsection}{0}\relax }\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Figure showing the 30 leading uncertainty sources in a fit of the $\sigma \mathcal  {B}$(cH(ZZ$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rightarrow 4\mu ))_{\kappa _c}$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}signal strength, ordered by their relative impact on the fit. In the left panel the nuisance parameter names are shown. In the central panel, the fitted nuisance parameter values as well as their so-called pulls are shown. On the right, the $\Delta \hat  {r}_i$ values are shown for the $\pm \sigma $ variations associated with each nuisance parameter.\relax }}{77}{figure.caption.62}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ImpactsSM}{{4.17}{77}{Figure showing the 30 leading uncertainty sources in a fit of the \sBR signal strength, ordered by their relative impact on the fit. In the left panel the nuisance parameter names are shown. In the central panel, the fitted nuisance parameter values as well as their so-called pulls are shown. On the right, the $\Delta \hat {r}_i$ values are shown for the $\pm \sigma $ variations associated with each nuisance parameter.\relax }{figure.caption.62}{}}
